WEBVTT

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.618
Journey seventeen: stay notified. A member receives events through the

00:00:05.618 --> 00:00:09.169
notification centre, toasts, banners and e-mail.

00:00:09.169 --> 00:00:13.904
This run takes the journey from eighty-nine percent to complete.

00:00:14.761 --> 00:00:19.823
This is the notification centre. One line is new in this run: New in

00:00:19.823 --> 00:00:25.183
Shops, Flower Creations. When a business is approved for the first time,

00:00:25.183 --> 00:00:30.097
members who browsed its category in the last ninety days are told.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:35.361
A notification that arrives while the app is open rises as a toast.

00:00:35.361 --> 00:00:40.262
It leaves after eight seconds, but while the pointer rests on it, or the

00:00:40.262 --> 00:00:42.440
focus is in it, the timer holds.

00:00:43.142 --> 00:00:48.347
Marking all as read fails here, and the error is an alert that stays

00:00:48.347 --> 00:00:52.098
until it is dismissed. At most three toasts show.

00:00:52.098 --> 00:00:56.078
A fourth pushes out the oldest, and never the error.

00:00:56.778 --> 00:01:01.521
Now the server refuses the event stream, and the browser gives up on it.

00:01:01.521 --> 00:01:06.066
The app opens the stream again by itself after five seconds, from the

00:01:06.066 --> 00:01:10.479
last event it received, so the next notification still arrives, and

00:01:10.479 --> 00:01:11.994
nothing is shown twice.

00:01:12.834 --> 00:01:17.355
In notification settings, How often now does what it says.

00:01:17.355 --> 00:01:21.954
The member chooses one daily summary, and it saves at once.

00:01:22.996 --> 00:01:27.400
From then on, e-mails about messages and requests are held.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:32.776
At seven in the morning, Toronto time, a job sends one e-mail that lists

00:01:32.776 --> 00:01:38.076
them in order. Meeting reminders and e-mails about sign-in and security

00:01:38.076 --> 00:01:39.196
are never held.

00:01:40.054 --> 00:01:43.702
Two more jobs send the e-mails a member can ask for.

00:01:43.702 --> 00:01:48.754
On Monday morning, a weekly note lists the businesses that joined in the

00:01:48.754 --> 00:01:53.595
categories a member browsed. On the first of the month, an owner of a

00:01:53.595 --> 00:01:56.542
public listing gets the next of four tips.

00:01:56.542 --> 00:01:59.278
Each carries Jubilee's mailing address.

00:02:00.087 --> 00:02:04.207
Every notification e-mail ends with an unsubscribe link.

00:02:04.207 --> 00:02:07.150
It now opens a page, without signing in.

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:12.448
The page turns that one category off, and says what stops and what still

00:02:12.448 --> 00:02:14.655
reaches the member in Jubilee.

00:02:15.359 --> 00:02:20.153
The link of a daily summary turns off new messages and request updates

00:02:20.153 --> 00:02:24.742
together, and the page says so. A link that does not verify changes

00:02:24.742 --> 00:02:26.591
nothing, and says that too.

00:02:27.623 --> 00:02:31.145
Behind the screen, the event stream is measured.

00:02:31.145 --> 00:02:36.354
In the API's tests, twenty notifications each reach an open stream, and

00:02:36.354 --> 00:02:40.463
ninety-five in a hundred must arrive within two seconds.

00:02:41.320 --> 00:02:46.052
Journey seventeen is complete. Left for the owner: set the mailing

00:02:46.052 --> 00:02:50.425
address in production, run the migration, measure delivery in

00:02:50.425 --> 00:02:52.648
production, and run the sweeps.
